WEBVTT
TITLE Group Homomorphisms Kernel And Quotient — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
An isomorphism renames the elements. A homomorphism is allowed to lose information: several elements may land on the same place.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Here is the map from Z6 to Z3 that takes each number to its remainder on division by three. Zero and three both land on zero.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
It still respects the operation: add first and then map, or map first and then add, and the answer is the same.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Two elements land on each image: six elements in, three out. The map is two to one, so it is not injective.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
That collapse is not a defect. It is the object: a homomorphism records what survives when a group is folded onto a smaller one.

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Definition: phi of a times b equals phi of a times phi of b, for every a and b. No bijection is required.

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
The identity goes to the identity. Apply the equation to the identity and the identity, and the image must behave as an identity.

8
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Inverses go to inverses, by applying the same equation to a and its inverse. Their product is the identity, and its image must be too.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Order is divided, not preserved. In Z4 to Z2 by remainder, the element one has order four and its image has order two.

10
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
That last line is the first trap. A homomorphism can shrink order; it can never create it, so the image order always divides the original order.

11
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.000
The kernel is the set of elements that land on the identity. In Z6 to Z3 by remainder, the kernel is the pair zero and three.

12
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
The kernel is always a subgroup: it holds the identity, it is closed, and it holds inverses. Here three plus three is zero, and three is its own inverse.

13
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Its size measures the collapse. A kernel of two on a group of six means every image has exactly two preimages.

14
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.000
A kernel of one element means nothing is lost. The kernel is the exact measure of how far the map is from being injective.

15
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
The kernel decides injectivity; the image does not. Image size tells you how much survived, not how much was merged.

16
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Claim: phi is injective exactly when its kernel is the single identity. Two directions, and both are short.

17
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Forward: if two elements have the same image, their quotient lands on the identity, so the quotient is in the kernel.

18
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Backward: if the kernel is trivial, phi of a equals phi of b forces a equals b. Only the identity can map to the identity.

19
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Z6 to Z3 has a kernel of two, so it is not injective: zero and three collide on zero. Z4 to Z4 by doubling has kernel zero and two.

20
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
On this page, 41 homomorphisms between small cyclic groups were tested, and injectivity matched a trivial kernel in every one.

21
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.000
The image is the set of values actually hit, and it is always a subgroup of the target. Here it is all of Z3.

22
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.000
For a finite group the sizes multiply exactly: the size of the group is the size of the kernel times the size of the image. Six is two times three.

23
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Z12 to Z4 by remainder: kernel three, image four, and twelve is three times four. So the kernel size is forced once the image is known.

24
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.000
The sign map from S3 to Z2: kernel three, image two, six is three times two. The kernel is the three rotations.

25
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
The image size always divides the order of the group, and the kernel size always divides it too. Both follow from the same counting.

26
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
The kernel is not just a subgroup. Conjugating any kernel element by any group element puts you back inside the kernel.

27
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
In D4, the eight symmetries of the square, there are ten subgroups and exactly six are normal. The four reflection pairs are not.

28
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.000
In S3 there are six subgroups and three normal: the identity, the three rotations, and the whole group.

29
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.000
Conjugating one transposition subgroup by the rotations gives all three of them, so no single one is closed under conjugation.

30
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.000
That is the counterexample: the identity and one reflection form a subgroup of D4, and it is the kernel of no homomorphism out of D4.

31
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.000
Take the cosets of the kernel: multiply the kernel by each element and collect. Here the three cosets are zero-three, one-four and two-five.

32
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Those three cosets form a group of three elements under coset multiplication, and it is the group Z3 again.

33
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Multiplication is well defined because the kernel is normal: a different representative gives the same coset. Twelve of the 36 ordered pairs share a coset, and all agree.

34
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
With a non-normal subgroup it fails. In S3, take the identity and the transposition (2 3). Multiply the identity by (1 2), then the transposition by (1 2).

35
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Same coset, different answer. That is exactly why the quotient is built on normal subgroups and not on subgroups.

36
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.000
The first isomorphism theorem: the group of cosets of the kernel is isomorphic to the image of the map.

37
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.000
The map sends a coset to the value its elements share. It is well defined, injective, onto the image, and it preserves the operation.

38
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Checked on Z6 to Z3: three cosets against three image values, and all nine coset products agree with the product in Z3.

39
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.000
And on S3 to Z2: two cosets, the rotations and the reflections, against the two values. Six is three times two.

40
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.000
This is the sentence the whole lesson builds to: every homomorphism is a quotient followed by an injective map.

41
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.000
Counting: a homomorphism out of Z_m is fixed by where the element one goes, and the choice must satisfy m times k equals zero modulo n.

42
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.000
The count is the greatest common divisor of m and n. For Z4 to Z4 the page enumerates 256 maps and finds four homomorphisms.

43
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:18.000
Z6 to Z3: 729 maps, three homomorphisms. The count is three, the greatest common divisor of six and three.

44
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.000
Onto homomorphisms exist only when n divides m, and then there are phi of n of them. Twelve to four gives two; six to four gives none.

45
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.000
For non-cyclic groups the count is not a formula. S3 to S3 is ten, S3 to Z3 is one, and S3 to V4 is four — all by enumeration.

46
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.000
GATE asks four shapes: is this map a homomorphism, how many are there, what is the kernel, and is it injective or onto.

47
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.000
The shortcut: the order of phi of a divides the order of a. Use it to rule a candidate out in one line before checking any product.

48
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Trap one: a homomorphism divides order, it does not preserve it. Trap two: the kernel is normal, so a non-normal subgroup is never a kernel.

49
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Trap three: the kernel size times the image size equals the group size. A pair of numbers that does not multiply out is wrong.

50
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
One line to carry: the kernel measures the collapse, the image is what survives, and the quotient joins them into one theorem.

